//! tr kodlu 1
// 37 — Settings from the command line: adjusting a program without touching its code.
// What it teaches: defining settings with defaults using command_line({...}), description and descriptions for the
// help text, changing a setting with --name value, --help.
// Builds on: dicts (11), none (03), text (04).
// Run (from the project root): tr examples/en/37_command_line_settings.tr
//   To change the settings: tr examples/en/37_command_line_settings.tr --step 500 --lr 0.1 --run trial2
//   For help:               tr examples/en/37_command_line_settings.tr --help

// command_line takes a dict: the keys are the setting names, the values their defaults. If --name value is not
// given on the command line the default is used; if it is given the value is converted to the type of the default.
A030 config = G801({Y500: 100, Y504: 0.01, run: "trial", log_file: A042},
    G910: "An example program that takes its settings from the command line",
    G911: {Y500: "number of training steps", Y504: "size of the step", run: "name of the run",
        log_file: "file the records are written to (nothing is written if none)"})

// The result is an object: a setting is read by its name.
G100("step:", config.Y500, "| learning rate:", config.Y504, "| run:", config.run)
G100("log file given:", config.log_file != A042)

// Settings are used in the code like ordinary variables.
A030 total_steps = config.Y500 * 2
G100("double the steps:", total_steps)

// --- Try it yourself ---
// 1. Run the program with --step 7 and see the output change.
// 2. Type --help: the descriptions and defaults are listed. Add a new setting and see it in the help.
